# Compositional synthesis: a conjunction decided and played part by part.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.
## Needs ltlsynt (from Spot) on the PATH.

# By default a conjunction is one synthesis problem. With ltl-compositional on,
# the parts of a conjunction that share no output are decided each for itself,
# and a run plays one strategy per part. The option is off by default:

get ltl-compositional
set ltl-compositional on

# Two parts here: o1 answers each i1, and o2 = 1 with o3 = 0 recurs on its own.
# The verdict is T because both parts are realizable, and the report counts
# the parts:

realizable (G (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> F o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (F (o2[t]:bv[1] = 1 && o3[t]:bv[1] = 0)))

# A run plays a strategy for each part. The inputs are those of a file:

i1:bv[1] := in file("fixture/lookback-bv1.in")
run 4 steps (G (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> F o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (F (o2[t]:bv[1] = 1 && o3[t]:bv[1] = 0)))
stop

# The verdict is the same with the option off, but the strategy is the one of
# the whole conjunction, which may choose other values. Here o2 alternates
# where the part strategy kept it at 1:

set ltl-compositional off
realizable (G (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> F o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (F (o2[t]:bv[1] = 1 && o3[t]:bv[1] = 0)))
i2:bv[1] := in file("fixture/lookback-bv1.in")
run 4 steps (G (i2[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> F o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (F (o2[t]:bv[1] = 1 && o3[t]:bv[1] = 0)))
stop

quit
